  on first login. On a successful response, [38;5;153mlogin[39m's caller stores the server-issued ID. Matches the existing no-build
  pattern exactly. Rough complexity: trivial.

  [1mApproach B — tracking-context object[0m
  [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, { userId } = {})[39m. Same module, but the third argument is a bag so later forms can add fields
  without another signature change. Buys extensibility; costs a slightly noisier call site and designs for fields that
  don't exist yet. Rough complexity: trivial.

  [1mApproach C — ES modules[0m
  Convert [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, make [38;5;153midentity.js[39m a real module with named exports, [38;5;153mimport[39m it in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
  Buys a proper import graph instead of load-order coupling on globals, and makes the module unit-testable without a DOM
  shim. Costs: touches the page's loading model, and [38;5;153mfile://[39m won't work anymore (modules need an origin, so it requires
  a local server). Rough complexity: moderate.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It's the honest read of what you asked for, it matches the repo's existing no-build convention,
  and the module boundary is what makes C a later refactor rather than a rewrite. I'd take C only if you're already
  planning to introduce a bundler or a dev server — the load-order coupling in A is real but it's one line of [38;5;153mindex.html
[39m  and one consumer.

  The extensibility B buys is the thing you'd want [3mif[0m the next form needs to pass more than an ID — and you'll know that
  when the form exists, at which point A converts to B in one edit.
